# mcp-server.dl — the MCP stdio server the +dl eval cells point at.
#
# The agent-eval harness gives a +dl cell exactly the built-in dl.what /
# dl.verb / dl.rows tools (src/mcp.rs's `builtin_tool_descriptors` /
# `builtin_tool_call`) and NOTHING else: no program-authored tool, no
# dl-specific hint text in the shared prompt (anti-grift protocol #2,
# plans/2026-07-10-agent-eval-harness.md). Those three tools are engine
# built-ins that any `--mcp` program advertises regardless of its own rules
# (`tools_list_merged` appends them; `tools/call` on a `dl.*` name is
# answered before the program's own dispatch ever runs), so this file needs
# no rules of its own — just the one @in(rpc)/@out(rpc) port pair `--mcp`
# requires to bind the profile at all (see examples/mcp-echo.dl).
#
#   dl bench/agent-eval/mcp-server.dl --mcp --no-daemon --db <scratch db>
#
# Run with cwd = the task's mutated worktree root (root is always cwd, never
# a flag — src/cli/root.rs), so dl.what/dl.verb/dl.rows read that worktree's
# own graph.
#
# FINDING (S1, filed against S0's --mcp adapter, not fixed here — see the
# harness report): a real MCP client (Claude Code itself, tested against
# this exact file with zero rules) sends the protocol-mandatory `initialize`
# handshake BEFORE ever asking for `tools/list`. src/mcp.rs's `handle_msg`
# only special-cases `tools/list` and `tools/call`; `initialize` falls
# through to the program's own rules, and a program with none gets a bare
# `-32601 no rule answered method \`initialize\`` — which every MCP client
# treats as a fatal connection failure, so the built-in dl.* tools never
# even reach the "advertised" stage. `examples/mcp-server.dl` already carries
# the fix as a one-line rule (below); a zero-logic "just serve the built-ins"
# deployment has to know to copy it. Candidate real fix (not this file, not
# in scope for S1): `--mcp` could answer a generic `initialize` itself,
# the way it already answers `tools/list`/`tools/call`, so a program needs
# zero lifecycle boilerplate to serve only built-ins.
rel req(id: text, method: text, params: text) @in(rpc).
rel resp(id: text, result: text) @out(rpc).

resp(id, "{\"protocolVersion\":\"2024-11-05\",\"capabilities\":{\"tools\":{}},\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"dl-agent-eval\",\"version\":\"0.1.0\"}}")
    <- req(id, "initialize", _).
